5: ## The course in brief
6: 
7: - **Units.** 4, one year of library and research skills, each unit one full inquiry cycle (ask, find, judge, use,
8:   share, reflect) on a small question, in the order a grade 4 class needs the moves: narrowing a topic into a plan and
9:   searching more than one kind of source first, then taking notes that answer from judged sources and using them
10:   honestly, then finding out what no source holds by asking others, then a free-choice inquiry of one's own, revised
11:   from advice.
12: - **Days.** 30: 26 in units, 2 course-level check days (1 after unit 2, 1 after unit 4) and 2 respond days (1 after
13:   each check). One session of about 35 minutes, one session a week, for 30 weeks (APPROACH.md, Pacing and dose: the
14:   3 to 5 default of 30 sessions of 35 minutes, about 17.5 hours a year, at the one-a-week end of the settled "one or
15:   two sessions a week"). The 35 minutes hold getting out notebooks and source sets, the session, putting books and
16:   cards back and collecting work; a lesson plan budgets about 3 minutes at each end for that, so about 29 working
17:   minutes, and every assessment form below is costed against those 29 minutes for a class of 30. One adult runs the
18:   room (the classroom teacher or a librarian). Nine grade 4 rows, seven of them owned (nine ids), fit 26 unit days by
19:   load: unit 1 carries two owned rows and gets 6 days; unit 2 three rows (two owned, three ids, the year's heaviest
20:   reading and writing) and gets 7; unit 3 two owned rows and gets 6; unit 4 two owned rows and the year's product,
21:   its feedback round and its revision, and gets 7. Each unit's days include its end-of-unit assessment (no unit is
22:   over 25 days, so no mid-unit assessment); units 1 to 3 also hold their respond day, and unit 4's respond step is
23:   its revision day, with its regrouped reteach in the year-end check's respond day. These are course days: with 30
24:   days over 30 weeks, course day n falls in week n of docs/courses/CALENDAR.md, and the scope and sequence places
25:   them on school days.
26: - **Day kinds.** In library's words (APPROACH.md, The kinds of day; docs/GLOSSARY.md, teaching day):
27:   Understanding is the show-me day (the teacher thinks aloud through the move on an invented case, holding the unit's
28:   skill card); Fluency is the practice day (short, varied tries of the same move on new cases, with feedback: narrow
29:   two topics, search one question three ways, sort four pages by purpose, rewrite closed questions as open ones);
30:   Application is the inquiry day (the class runs part of the cycle on its host question: plan, search, judge, note,
31:   make, share). Fluency is modest in grade 4: one practice day in units 1 and 2, a practice half-day in units 2 and 3, plus the five-minute opening
32:   retrieval on later days. Every unit's day 1 is a show-me day with the hook and the compelling question.
33: - **Standard.** AASL National School Library Standards, second edition, learner framework (ids and our own words
34:   only, data/standards/aasl-words.txt; never the competency text, and no claim of alignment approved by anyone).
35:   Ownership rule (STANDARD.md section 3): an id is owned once, in the school year where it is first checked alone.
36:   Grade 4 owns nine ids (AASL.I.B.2; AASL.IV.B.1; AASL.I.B.1 and AASL.IV.B.4; AASL.VI.A.2; AASL.V.C.2; AASL.II.D.2;
37:   AASL.V.A.3; AASL.I.C.3) and carries nine as `~`, each named in its unit's What students learn with its owner.
38:   Skills: the nine progression rows, each in one unit. Check set: the Empire State Information Fluency Continuum, 3
39:   to 5 band file, grade 4 column and its bold priority skills (read cell by cell for this frame, later.csv L103;
40:   result in the Check set read bullet); the "Check set adds" cells name what each unit plan should weigh from it.
41:   Values: K to 5 case sets stay neutral (no politics, elections, religion, identity groups or conflict; Cory,
42:   2026-11-21).
43: - **Base text.** None. Every day is Curio Class's own. Curio Class writes: a short background passage and a source set
44:   for each unit's stand-alone question (passages at grade 4 level, a photo with caption, a log or letter, a diagram),
45:   an invented printed catalog page, an invented children's encyclopedia entry and an invented results page for unit 1,
46:   four invented web pages with differing purposes (one with an ad), the honest-use case cards, the survey and
47:   observation plan sheets, the interview record sheet, the "I found out" sheet, the change log, one skill card a unit
48:   and the research notebook pages. No day needs a trade book, a paid source, a library visit or a database (no library
49:   access is assumed; Cory, 2026-11-11).
50: - **Host tasks.** Every unit runs its cycle on a host task from another grade 4 course, named by its row id for the
51:   teacher to schedule against, and carries a stand-alone host question of its own (an invented question with a closed
52:   source set) so the unit runs when no core task is in step or at home (APPROACH.md). The scope and sequence records
53:   both. A host is used for its question only when its topic keeps the K to 5 neutral rule; otherwise the unit borrows
