170: ## Units
171: 
172: | # | Unit and compelling question | Days | Skills | Owned standards | Inquiry phases | What students learn | Why this unit, here | Check set adds | Sensitive topics and teacher notes |
173: |---|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|
174: | 1 | Planning a research path. *How do we turn a big topic into a plan, and which kinds of source help most?* | 6 | g4-narrow-and-plan; g4-search-more-kinds | AASL.I.B.2; AASL.IV.B.1 | Connect, wonder, investigate (search), reflect | Picking an interesting part of a broad topic, writing two or three questions about it with a predicted answer, and making a research plan: what they still need to know, which sources they will try and in what order (AASL.I.B.2, owned here: "Plan the steps and sources that will fill what you still do not know," checked alone). Searching the printed catalog page, an invented children's encyclopedia entry and an invented results page for the same question with the same key words, using contents, index and glossary inside a book to reach the answering part, and recording what each kind gave (AASL.IV.B.1, owned here: "Search more than one kind of source, not just the first site," checked alone). Revisited: turning a topic into research questions (AASL.I.A.1, owned by g3-research-questions, grade 3); using information and devices responsibly (AASL.VI.A.1, owned by g3-safe-searching, grade 3), the stop rule kept on every search. Out: changing a query on a results page and skipping ads (grade 5, g5-search-revise); filters. Opening retrieval: day 2, three questions on a picture, one marked thick (g3-research-questions); day 3, key words and a synonym for one question and one scenario card (g3-key-words, g3-safe-searching). Days: show-me (hook; narrowing a broad topic to a part, the plan with a predicted answer), show-me (one question searched three ways; what each kind is good for; contents and index), practice (narrow two topics; search one question three ways and record), inquiry (plan and search for the host question, a partner reads the plan, the self-check), end-of-unit assessment, respond. Skill card: "big topic, my part, questions, guess, plan; same words, three kinds, what each gave". End-of-unit evidence (written sitting, about 25 minutes for 30): from a new broad topic and a short overview passage, a narrowed part, two or three questions with a predicted answer, and a plan whose sources fit its questions (rubric); then a search plan for one of the questions across two of three new printed kinds, saying what each was good for (rubric). Tools: the Arctic overview passage, the three printed kinds, the plan sheet and search plan (Curio Class writes all). Host: the neighboring-country research of social studies grade 4 unit 2 (days 17 to 38) (ss4-canada-mexico-research), on its land, climate and animals; stand-alone: "How do Arctic animals get through the winter?" | First because every later unit starts from a plan and a search across kinds; the weeks fit social studies grade 4's research project on a neighboring country, which needs a narrowed part and a plan | Candidate: the continuum's grade 4 cells on recognizing gaps in what one knows and stating what is known from an overview, at the plan's first line; following a template and a timeline, as the plan sheet | The host keeps to land, climate, animals and places; no question about a people, a culture or a border issue (K to 5 neutral rule). Searches use the printed kinds or a teacher-checked page under the stop rule (APPROACH.md, Sensitive ground, item 2). Whose voices: the unit records the makers of its sources (C12) |
175: | 2 | Notes that answer. *What do beavers really do to a stream, and how do we use what sources say honestly?* | 7 | g4-evidence-notes; g4-primary-secondary; g4-honest-use | AASL.I.B.1; AASL.IV.B.4; AASL.VI.A.2 | Investigate (sources and evidence), construct, reflect | Reading a source with the research question beside it and noting only the facts that answer it, in one's own words, crossing out "interesting but not an answer" facts and marking what is opinion as opinion (AASL.I.B.1, owned here: "Look for evidence that answers your research question, not just related facts"); sorting notes into categories the student makes and can explain (AASL.IV.B.4, owned here: "Sort notes by topic, importance or another plan you can explain"; both checked alone). Comparing a primary and a secondary source the teacher chose on the same topic, saying what each can tell that the other cannot, and sorting invented pages by purpose (inform, persuade, sell, entertain), finding the ad on a page. Explaining in one's own words what honest use means: putting an idea in your own words, using a few exact words in quotation marks with the source, saying where it came from, and why a maker cares; sorting cases into honest and not yet honest and fixing the second kind (AASL.VI.A.2, owned here: "Explain what fair and honest use of information and media means," checked alone). Begun earlier, owned later: questioning a source's maker and purpose (AASL.IV.B.3, owned by g5-first-lateral-check, grade 5); choosing which sources to use (AASL.IV.A.3, owned by g5-choose-sources, grade 5). Revisited: naming who made each work (AASL.VI.B.2, owned by g2-credit-line, grade 2), as the source list on the note sheet. Out: notes from three sources marked for agreement (grade 5, g5-evidence-notes); note cards keeping paraphrase and quotation apart (grade 6). Opening retrieval: day 2, two key-word notes from a short paragraph (g3-notes-in-boxes); day 3, label two sources and name each maker (g3-source-kinds-and-makers); day 4, a source list line for one cover (g3-source-list). Days: show-me (answer-only notes and one's own categories), show-me (primary and secondary on the same topic; purpose and the ad), show-me with practice (honest use: own words, a short quotation, credit; sort and fix four cases), practice (notes from a new passage into own boxes; four invented pages sorted by purpose), inquiry (notes from a primary and a secondary source for the host question into the student's own boxes, a source list, a pair share, the self-check), end-of-unit assessment, respond. Skill card: "question beside me, answer only, my words, my boxes; first-hand or about it, why made; my words, a few in marks, say where". End-of-unit evidence (written sitting, about 27 minutes for 30): a new passage and question, notes that keep only the answers, in own words, sorted into the student's own labeled boxes with the plan said (rubric, about 12 minutes); one line on what a new primary and a new secondary source can each tell, and three invented pages sorted by purpose with the ad marked (items, about 7 minutes); two not-yet-honest cases fixed with a reason (rubric, about 8 minutes). Tools: the beaver source set (a wildlife worker's field log, a photo with caption, two book passages), four invented pages, the honest-use case cards (Curio Class writes all). Host: the energy resources question of science grade 4 unit 2 (days 16 to 38) (s4-energy-resources), or the animal defenses research of ELA grade 4 unit 2 (days 39 to 77) (e4-research), in the same weeks; stand-alone: "What do beavers do to a stream when they build a dam?" | After unit 1 because a plan and a search have put sources in hand, and notes must now keep only what answers the plan's questions; honest use sits with note-making, where copying starts; before unit 3, whose records and unit 4's sheet are built from such notes | Candidate: the continuum's grade 4 cells on facts, details and inferences that support a main idea and a conclusion drawn from evidence, at the notes step; noting where two sources differ, as one line of the inquiry day | Honest use without shaming (APPROACH.md, Sensitive ground, item 4): a student whose notes copy a sentence is helped privately to put it in their own words and cite, never named in front of the class. Social studies grade 4 unit 4 uses primary sources on voyages and conquest; library borrows only the primary and secondary idea and runs on beaver cases (K to 5 neutral rule). The ad page sells an invented product with no brand. Whose voices: the unit records the makers of its sources (C12) |
176: | 3 | When no book has the answer. *How can we find out something no source can tell us yet?* | 6 | g4-class-survey; g4-asking-others | AASL.V.C.2; AASL.II.D.2 | Wonder, investigate (with others), construct, express, reflect | When no source holds the answer, a group works out a new way to find out: a class survey on a neutral question (which of three read-aloud books the class wants to hear again) or an observation plan (how the schoolyard soil changes after rain, over a week), writes the steps, collects and tallies (AASL.V.C.2, owned here: "Work out with others a new way to find something out," checked alone: each student explains the plan and why it answers). Planning three open questions to ask a classmate or a school adult how they see the research topic, asking them, listening without arguing and recording the answer (AASL.II.D.2, owned here: "Ask others how they see a topic and show you want to know," checked alone). Revisited: speaking up with findings in a group (AASL.III.D.1) and naming what others add (AASL.II.A.1), both owned by g3-research-team, grade 3. Out: widening a question by researching together (grade 5, g5-group-inquiry); a survey of anyone outside the class. A tally and a bar graph use grade 3 math (math grade 3 unit 1 (days 1 to 22), scaled graphs). Opening retrieval (day 2): name what each of three sample team members added (g3-research-team). Days: show-me (hook; a question no book answers; a survey or an observation plan and its steps), show-me with practice (open and closed questions, listening and recording; rewrite four closed questions as open and plan the steps for one case), inquiry (groups run the survey or start the observation, which runs for a week between sessions; pairs ask a classmate or a school adult their three questions and record), inquiry with share (tally and say what the plan found; the self-check), end-of-unit assessment, respond. Skill card: "no source? plan a new way, steps, collect, tally; open question, listen, record". End-of-unit evidence (written sitting, about 22 minutes for 30): each student explains in writing the group's plan and why it answers the question (rubric); then three open questions for a new neutral topic, and a record of a short answer the teacher reads aloud once (rubric: questions open, record matches what was said). Tools: the survey and observation plan sheets, the tally sheet, the interview record sheet (Curio Class writes all). Host: an observation for the changing land unit of science grade 4 unit 4 (days 57 to 73), for example how rain moves soil in the schoolyard; stand-alone: "Which read-aloud book does our class most want to hear again?" | After unit 2 because students can now tell when the sources they judged do not hold an answer; the year's middle is when a class can run a week-long observation between sessions; before unit 4, where a student may use a survey or an asked adult for their own question | Candidate: the continuum's grade 4 cells on working with a group to research, decide and make a shared result, and on restating another's idea accurately before adding one's own, at the survey and the interview record | Surveys and asking people (APPROACH.md, Sensitive ground, item 8): questions stay on a neutral class topic and are checked by the teacher before use; no question about family, home, beliefs, identity, health, money, food or meals; surveys are of the class, with no names recorded; any school adult may be asked, inside school; the answers are reported without a full name. The observation is from a window or a set spot with the teacher. Whose voices: the unit records who answered (by role, never by name) (C12) |
177: | 4 | A question of my own. *What do I want to find out, just to learn, and how can advice make my answer better?* | 7 | g4-own-question; g4-revise-from-advice | AASL.V.A.3; AASL.I.C.3 | Connect, wonder, investigate, construct, express, reflect | A short free-choice inquiry: each student picks a question of their own from a neutral list or their own wonder (how do bees make honey?), researches it just to learn with the year's moves (a plan, a search across kinds, answer-only notes, a source list, as review), names who the sheet is for before choosing its form, and makes a one-page "I found out" sheet (AASL.V.A.3, owned here: "Research a question of your own just to learn more about it," checked alone: the question is the student's own and the sheet answers it). After a feedback round, choosing which advice to use, making the changes and keeping a short change log (advice, what I changed, or why I kept it) (AASL.I.C.3, owned here: "Use the advice you get to revise your own research product," checked alone). Revisited: saying what one wonders (AASL.V.C.1, owned by k-wonder, kindergarten); giving specific, kind advice (AASL.I.C.2, owned by g3-gallery-feedback, grade 3), in the feedback round. Out: sharing with a real audience beyond the class (grade 5, g5-share-real-audience). Opening retrieval: day 2, find the sentence in a sample product not backed by its notes (g3-make-product); day 4, one specific, kind suggestion on a sample sheet (g3-gallery-feedback). Days: show-me (hook; choosing a question of one's own; the audience line), inquiry (plan and search), inquiry (notes and the draft sheet with its source list), feedback round (pairs read and leave advice on the skill card), show-me with revision (choosing advice, the change log, revising), end-of-unit assessment, share and self-check (the sheets are shared in a class gallery, about 15 minutes, then a regrouped reteach). Skill card: "my question, my plan, my sheet; advice, choose, change, log". End-of-unit evidence (about 27 minutes for 30): the revised sheet and its change log are scored on their rubric (the question is the student's own, the sheet answers it, the log matches the changes), with the draft day, the written advice and the revision counted in the unit's days above; the written sitting, all at once, gives a new sample sheet with three pieces of advice: the student chooses which to use, writes the log lines and makes one change (rubric, about 15 minutes). Tools: the neutral question list, the "I found out" sheet, the change log, a general source set of short passages on the list's topics (Curio Class writes all). Host: the writing-process revision of ELA grade 4 unit 1 (days 1 to 38) (e4-writing-process), used as the class's feedback habit; stand-alone: each student's own question from the neutral list | Last because it hands the whole cycle to the student: a plan and search (unit 1), notes and honest use (unit 2) and, where no source answers, asking others (unit 3) are what a student brings to a question of their own; the revision needs a finished sheet | Candidate: the continuum's grade 4 cells on choosing a format for an audience and a purpose, and on presenting with care for content and delivery, at the share; identifying what makes a good product, as the rubric read before drafting | Questions stay on neutral ground in K to 5: the teacher steers a sensitive choice privately to a nearby neutral one, never in front of the class (the row's note). Advice stays kind and about the work; the teacher reads the slips before they reach their owner. Whose voices: the unit records the makers of its sources (C12) |
