179: ## Course-level checks
180: 
181: | Check | After unit | Check days | Respond days | What it checks |
182: |---|---|---|---|---|
183: | 1, mid-year | 2 | 1 | 1 | Week 14, one session, a written sitting, about 25 minutes for 30. It samples unit 1 on a new case: a narrowed part of a new broad topic, two questions with a predicted answer and a plan whose sources fit them; a search plan across two new printed kinds saying what each was good for. Unit 2, which ended the week before, is not re-tested: its end-of-unit result is reused unscored, as a class count of the students who met each rubric step, written on this check's sheet. The respond day (week 15) reteaches the missed step in small groups from the sheet, ending with one fresh try |
184: | 2, year-end | 4 | 1 | 1 | Week 29, one session, a written sitting, about 27 minutes for 30. It samples units 1 to 3 on new cases: a plan for a new topic (unit 1); answer-only notes from a short passage into the student's own boxes, three invented pages sorted by purpose and one not-yet-honest case fixed (unit 2); three open questions for a new topic and one line on why a survey or an observation answers a question no source holds (unit 3). Unit 4, which ended in week 28, is reused unscored as a class count on the sheet, as at mid-year. The respond day (week 30) reteaches the gaps in small groups from the sheet, then holds the year's reflect step: each student writes one thing they would like to learn more about, one step they now do well and one to work on in grade 5 |
