 on the canal's supporters and opponents; the myths' quests and trials compared across three cultures on the narrative arc (RL.4.9), and the words they gave English (Herculean, a quest; the allusion part of RL.4.4, e4-lit-word-meaning, unit 1). The myths sit here because each is a journey against great labor, read beside the canal's own labor. The play, read aloud and then performed, compared with its script (review of e4-poetry-drama, RL.4.7). Writing: a piece urging the people of 1817 to support or oppose the canal, reasons each backed by facts and details from the texts, grouped, linked (for instance, in order to, in addition) and concluded (W.4.1a to d), drawing evidence from the history texts (W.4.9b, used again), through the process with the compound-sentence comma taught in editing (L.4.2c); a short first-person scene of a person of the canal's path written in one sitting (review of e4-narrative); a two-sided discussion where both sides are defensible. Grammar continued from unit 2 on the canal texts: progressive tenses (L.4.1b, "the crews were digging"), prepositional phrases (L.4.1e, "along the route") and confused words (L.4.1g, to, too, two; there, their). Review: e4-text-structure (the timeline and cause and effect), e4-visual-info (the timeline and a map of the route), e4-integrate-two | The base text's module 3 design (a history set, a play of a family divided, a piece urging a side, a structured discussion) moved off the American Revolution to an event grade 4 social studies teaches (the progression stitch). It needs unit 2's structures and integration, and the opinion piece needs the reasons-and-evidence work it opens. Three days inside the unit become myth days (the source map names which); the unit stays 39 days | Candidate: rubric 2e (texts from several cultures, each credited), 2v (an end task joining reading, writing and speaking), 2b (texts of the period with a reason for each) | Haudenosaunee lands taken for the canal: named plainly, the Nations named, the unlawful purchases named, never framed as empty land; the sensitivity agent checks the history and any Haudenosaunee voice against the Nations' own published accounts (brief). The laborers' danger and illness named without graphic detail. Students argue a side in writing and discussion as themselves looking back, never in role as a person of the time; no simulation. Myths credited to the people who tell them. Flagged for the sensitivity read (the study named in the brief) |
| 4 | The vote. "How did people who could not vote win it, and who was left out?" | 37 | e4-firsthand-secondhand; e4-formal-informal | RI.4.6; SL.4.6; L.4.3 | Not used (ELA). Leans on social studies grade 4 (primary sources) | Anchors: an original Curio Class chapter book read whole (a present-day girl finds family letters from 1920 and follows a great-great-aunt's work for the vote; about 15,000 to 25,000 words, measure target about 800 to 930, the lower half of the band); Harry T. Burn's August 19, 1920 explanation of his ratifying vote in the Tennessee House Journal (page 94; public domain), beside a later secondhand account of the same vote; federal archive pages on the amendment and its signers (public domain); short pieces naming the Black women who worked for the vote and were shut out of parts of the movement, and that many could not vote after 1920; original articles on present-day kids who made a difference in their towns. A firsthand and a secondhand account of the Tennessee vote compared on focus and information, and why they differ (RI.4.6). Formal English when presenting and informal in a small group, told apart and used (SL.4.6, L.4.3c); precise words (L.4.3a) and punctuation for effect (L.4.3b) chosen in revision. Grammar continued on sentences from the chapter book and drafts: relative pronouns and adverbs (L.4.1a, "who", "whose", "where"), with the year's other parts (1b to 1g) checked again in editing. Research on how kids have made a difference (review of e4-research), and the year's spoken and written products: a short public service announcement recorded as audio on a classroom device, and a press release in formal English set against a note to a classmate. Review: e4-theme-summary and e4-lit-word-meaning (idioms in the chapter book's dialogue), e4-reasons-evidence (speeches for the vote), e4-opinion (a piece on how students can make a difference), e4-presenting, e4-grammar | The base text's module 4: last because it joins research, opinion and presenting, and it previews grade 5's suffrage work in social studies (kept by the progression stitch). It owns few new rows: its 37 days are the base module's practice, the year's second long text (the chapter book, at the band's upper half) and the year-end products, lesson for lesson. One day fewer: two lessons of class action are merged into one | Candidate: rubric 2m and 2n (grammar from the anchor, then in the student's own editing), 2p (writing in formal and informal registers) | Racism inside the suffrage movement and Black women's exclusion named plainly, with people of the time named as they named themselves; previewed. The class action stays a message the class chooses (an announcement or a press release); no student is asked to act on a cause of the teacher's choosing. Flagged for the sensitivity read (the study named in the brief) |

