| # | Unit and compelling question | Days | Skills | Owned standards | C3 | What students learn | Why this unit, here | Check set adds | Sensitive topics and teacher notes |
|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|
| 1 | Faster and harder. "Why does a faster ball do more when it hits something?" Anchor: marbles rolled down a cardboard ramp into a paper cup: the faster marble sends the cup farther and makes a louder clack | 15 | s4-speed-energy; s4-collisions | 4-PS3-1; 4-PS3-3 | Practices: constructing explanations (speed and energy); asking questions and predicting outcomes (collisions). Crosscutting: energy and matter; cause and effect | Energy as something a moving object has more of when it moves faster: a faster ball knocks a cup farther, a faster cart makes a louder bump; speed compared (faster, slower), never calculated; a ramp's height only a way to get more speed; how far the cup slides measured in whole centimetres over several trials at each start height and shown on a line plot (grade 2's line plots), so the fair test and repeated trials are taught early. Questions and predictions about collisions: a rolling marble hits a still one, a toy car hits a block; the faster one before gives more motion, sound or warmth after. The energy transfer model starts here as simple arrows from where the energy was before the crash to every place it went after (the cup, the sound, a little warmth); grade 3's force arrows appear only for the push at contact. The change in energy is tied to the change in speed | Energy opens the year because units 2 and 3 follow the same arrows; the two rows share one anchor, as the progression pairs them, and collisions come before energy transfer (the rows' order) so the arrows are built on something students can watch | Candidate: the sound of a collision recorded at two speeds as a second form of evidence | Rolling objects at low speed on the floor or with a table-edge guard; no throwing. Boundary: no number measurements of energy; stored energy of position is not taught or tested. Retrieval task on grade 3's motion patterns. Simulation to list: a ramp-and-cup collision tool for the no-kit path. Agent reads: sensitivity; safety (hands-on). |
| 2 | Energy on the move. "How does energy get from place to place, and where does the energy we use come from?" Anchor: a hand-crank flashlight that lights when the handle turns, with no battery to change | 23 | s4-energy-transfer; s4-energy-device; s4-energy-resources | 4-PS3-2; 4-PS3-4; 4-ESS3-1 | Practices: planning and carrying out investigations (transfer); constructing explanations and designing solutions (device); obtaining, evaluating and communicating information (resources). Crosscutting: energy and matter; cause and effect | Observations that give evidence energy moves by sound (a drum makes rice on another drum jump), light (sunlight warms a dark cloth), heat (a warm hand warms a cold spoon) and electric current (a battery, wires and a bulb or buzzer in a closed circuit); the arrows extended through all four. Current gets its own hands-on days (about three: light a bulb, trace the closed path, add a switch) before the build, so every student has wired a circuit whatever device a team later picks. A device that changes energy from one form to another, designed, tested and refined: the class states criteria and constraints, compares first proposals, builds, finds where it fails and improves it. Named pick: a lid alarm (a circuit that sounds a buzzer when a box lid opens); the clarification's other examples (a rubber-band car using stored energy, a passive solar heater) are offered as team choices. Then the question the device raises: where does the current in the wall come from? Energy and fuels from natural resources: renewable (wind, water behind dams, sunlight) and nonrenewable (coal, oil, natural gas, and the fuel of nuclear power plants), and how using them affects the environment (habitat lost to dams and surface mines, air pollution from burning fossil fuels), from texts and the class's own state's energy data, shown as a bar graph or picture graph ordered by size ("of every 10 lights lit in our state, about 4 run on natural gas"), with no percent and no number over 1,000,000 | Follows unit 1's arrows. Transfer comes before the device (it needs circuits and forms of energy), and resources come right after the device, opened by the question it raises (the row's order). Placed before school day 107 so social studies grade 4's regions-and-resources unit can lean on it. 23 days: three rows, one of them a build | Candidate kept (day 34, one day): the class's state energy profile (public federal data) as the local case | Low-voltage batteries only, never outlets; the bulb or buzzer always in the loop; watch for hot wires; solar heaters can get hot, check with a thermometer, not a hand; no lenses aimed at skin. Readiness day 15 on sunlight warming, last explicitly taught in kindergarten; response inside day 18. The day 16 hook stays together. Resources: some students' families work in coal, oil, gas, wind or solar; facts and backed sources decide claims (values rule b) and no resource or job is cheered or blamed. Budgets for the device use whole-number addition and subtraction (see Cross-subject). Simulation to list: a circuit builder for the no-kit path. Agent reads: sensitivity; safety (hands-on). |
| 3 | Waves and messages. "What do waves carry, and how can a pattern carry a message?" Anchor: a cork on a tub of water bobs up and down as waves pass, but goes nowhere; and the class cannot shout a message across the playing field | 15 | s4-wave-model; s4-information-codes | 4-PS4-1; 4-PS4-3; 3-5-ETS1-2 | Practices: developing and using models (waves); constructing explanations and designing solutions (codes; generate and compare several solutions). Crosscutting: patterns | A model of waves (a rope or coiled-spring wave, a straw-and-tape wave machine, gentle repeating dips at one end of a large tub of water, not a small tray, whose reflected ripples hide the pattern) to describe them by amplitude (how tall) and wavelength (peak to peak), and to show that waves make things move (the cork) without carrying them away; students compare gentle repeating waves of different amplitude, and of longer and shorter wavelength, watch what each does to a floating cork, and revise their model to explain the difference in its motion (a taller wave lifts it higher), wavelength compared qualitatively in words and with an unnumbered strip, no numbers or equations; sound, built on grade 1's vibrations, shown with the coiled spring pushed and pulled along its length (bunched and spread coils travel along it) beside the rope or water wave as a second kind, both carrying energy without carrying the material away, with no names for the two kinds of wave; amplitude and wavelength stay on the rope and water models; a wave carries energy across while the water stays where it was. Then several solutions that use patterns to send information. Before designing, students investigate the communication problem (the shout that does not carry) and read one short text on how information is encoded and decoded: digitized information sent over long distances without losing quality, as phones and computers turn voice or a picture into a pattern and back. Then drum beats in a code across the field, a grid of 1s and 0s that sends a black and white picture, flashlight Morse code: each team generates at least two codes, shares its proposals with another team, tests the same message under two conditions (near and far, or quiet and noisy), records accuracy and one other agreed measure (time to send), and compares the codes on those data against shared criteria (speed, errors, distance) and constraints. 3-5-ETS1-2 in full; planning a fair test of a prototype stays grade 5's | Needs unit 2's energy and sound. The wave model comes first (the row's order) so sound signals can be described with it; codes teach the compare-several-solutions step that unit 5 reuses on hazards | None (the string-and-cup telephone candidate was dropped; the scope and sequence gives it zero days) | Water trays on covered tables; wipe spills at once. Light signals use patterns of flashes; no wave model of light at this grade. The code rule is stated and followed; the features of a number pattern wait for math grade 4 unit 7 (see Cross-subject). The days are tight for both rows: the scope and sequence sets the research and the two-condition test inside the 15 days. Boundary: no interference, no electromagnetic waves, no number models of amplitude or wavelength. Readiness day 41 on grade 1 vibrations only; signal devices are retrieved on day 43, alongside the vibration response. Wavelength drawn and compared with an unnumbered strip, never measured in numbers; point, line and segment named informally until math teaches them (see Cross-subject). Simulation to list: a wave tank tool. Agent reads: sensitivity; safety (hands-on). |
| 4 | The changing land. "How did a river make a canyon a mile deep, and what do its layers tell us?" Anchor: the Grand Canyon: a small river at the bottom of a canyon a mile deep, and the same stripes of rock on both walls (a teacher may swap in a local road cut or river bank) | 17 | s4-weathering-erosion; s4-rock-layers | 4-ESS2-1; 4-ESS1-1 | Practices: planning and carrying out investigations (weathering and erosion rates); constructing explanations (rock layers). Crosscutting: cause and effect; patterns; stability and change | The canyon question first goes outside, to change the class can see: the bare patch under a downspout after a hard rain (a channel and a fan of sand, the grass beside it untouched) and a cracked step or a path lifted by a root, sketched and kept apart as erosion and weathering (a photograph set where the class cannot go out). Observations and measurements of weathering (breaking rock into smaller pieces: freezing and thawing a wet clay or plaster block; shaking rocks in a jar) and of the rate of erosion (carrying the pieces away) by water, ice, wind and plants: a sloped tray of sand with slope, water amount or plant cover changed one at a time; data in tables and bar graphs. The two words never blurred. Rainfall shapes the land and affects what can live there; living things change the land (roots crack rock, burrows move soil). Then rock layers and their fossils as evidence of how a landscape changed, opened by a retrieval task on grade 3's fossils: shell fossils in a layer above plant fossils show land became sea; the canyon shows a river cut through the rock over a long time; lower layers in undisturbed rock are older (relative time); a layer model of modeling clay with shells or buttons buried as fossils, cut and read, then pushed from both ends so it bends and breaks; one real case of bent or broken layers read as evidence of earth forces. Nature of science: the record is read by assuming the same processes worked then as now | Weathering and erosion come before rock layers (the row's order: the canyon is erosion over a long time). It needs grade 3's fossils and the data habits of grade 3's weather unit. It sits before unit 5, whose flood solutions are tested on this unit's erosion tray | Candidate: a local landform (a gully, a beach, a road cut) visited or photographed as the class's own evidence | Water and sand on covered tables; wash hands after soil. BASELINE rule 5: the great age of the Earth stated as the evidence shows. Boundary: one kind of weathering or erosion at a time; not how rocks form; no named formations to memorize; relative time only. Erosion-readiness day 56, response day 59; long-time retrieval day 57 from grade 3 fossils, weather-data retrieval day 62, erosion retrieval day 65 and fossil retrieval day 67. Distances in whole centimetres; water measured in equal cupfuls (a nonstandard equal unit, as grade 3 counted washers) until math grade 4 unit 6 teaches millilitres and conversions in about the same weeks. Simulation to list: a stream-table tool for the no-kit path. Agent reads: sensitivity; safety (hands-on). |
| 5 | Earth's patterns and hazards. "Where do earthquakes and volcanoes happen, and how can people there stay safer?" Anchor: a world map of one year's earthquakes and of volcanoes erupted or possibly active within the past 10,000 years: the dots make lines, not a scatter | 17 | s4-earth-feature-maps; s4-hazard-solutions | 4-ESS2-2; 4-ESS3-2 | Practices: analyzing and interpreting data (maps); constructing explanations and designing solutions (hazards). Crosscutting: patterns; cause and effect | A short grade 2 review first (s2-water-where): a map locating the major ice-covered areas beside the oceans and land, so grade 5 can graph how much water each holds. Then topographic maps of land and the ocean floor (opened with a clay mountain sliced into layers and traced, so contour lines mean something) and maps of mountains, continental edges, volcanoes and earthquakes, with the map data first put into a table or chart, then the pattern described: mountains, volcanoes and earthquakes line up in belts, often along continents' edges, and the ocean floor has mountain ranges and trenches; why is left as a question grade 6 answers. Then several solutions that reduce the effects of natural Earth processes on people, generated and compared against criteria: an earthquake-resistant building tested on a shake table (a tray on rubber balls), better monitoring of a volcano, tsunami warnings, flood walls, channels and raised houses tested on the unit 4 erosion tray under a small and then a large flood, so each solution is tested under more than one likely condition; the maps show where each hazard clusters. People cannot stop these hazards; they can reduce the harm, and the comparison is judged on that | Needs unit 3's compare-several-solutions step and unit 4's erosion tray. Maps before hazards (the rows' order) so students know where hazards cluster. Ends by about school day 117 so social studies grade 4's hazards unit (from school day 137) can lean on it | Candidate: one recent earthquake or eruption from public USGS data, read for where it fell on the belt | Some students may have lived through a disaster: examples kept general, no student asked to recount one, sharing the student's choice. Shake table: no climbing, small loads. Boundary: earthquakes, floods, tsunamis and volcanic eruptions only; no plate tectonics by name. Readiness day 76 on grade 2 water locations only; response day 78, off the hook day, which also retrieves the grade 2 land-water model. Simulation to list: a map plotter of public earthquake and volcano data. Agent reads: sensitivity; safety (hands-on). |
| 6 | Senses and structures. "Can a cat see in complete darkness, and how does a plant that cannot move get water, stay safe and spread its young?" Anchor A: a cat crossing a yard at night: its eyes shine in a flashlight or a photo flash, it walks where we would stumble, and it bolts at a sound we did not hear (a photograph or clip, or a pet at home). Anchor B: a plant cannot walk to water, run from what eats it or carry its young, yet a cut white flower stood in colored water has colored petals by morning, and one dandelion seeds a field Anchor: the cat at night (eyes that shine in a flash, sure feet where we stumble, a bolt at a sound we missed) and the cut white flower in colored water whose petals turn colored by morning | 24 | s4-light-eye; s4-senses-brain; s4-structures-function | 4-PS4-2; 4-LS1-2; 4-LS1-1 | Practices: developing and using models (light; senses); engaging in argument from evidence (structures). Crosscutting: cause and effect; systems and system models | A model that we see an object because light from a source bounces off it into the eye: drawn rays from lamp to object to eye, tested with mirrors, a flashlight in a box with a peephole and a dark room; the common idea that light goes out from the eye tested and revised, and the cat's eye shine explained as light from the flash reflected back, not light the eye makes; the dark box answers whether anything sees with no light at all. Senses: a flow model from sense organ to brain to action, tested with a dropped-ruler reaction game and other animals (an owl hearing prey, a snake sensing heat); animals use perceptions and memories to guide actions, shown with a new clap pattern that comes to mean a set action, counted in early and late rounds. Structures: an argument that internal and external structures support survival, growth, behavior and reproduction (roots, thorns, colored petals, heart, lungs, stomach, brain, skin), each part of a larger system; the plant half argued as fully as the animal half, opened by anchor B: external structures from a case set (a cactus's thick stem, spines and wide shallow roots; thorns; colored petals; the dandelion's seed parachutes); an internal plant case required, a celery stalk or white flower stem cut by an adult and stood in colored water, whose color rising through the tubes shows the inside structures that carry water from roots to leaves, roots, tubes and leaves argued as parts working together; a flower dissection (petals, stamens, pistil) required as the plant reproduction case; the unit's end task asks for one plant argument and one animal argument; the argument judged for whether its evidence is relevant and enough | Needs unit 2's light as a form of energy, so it follows the energy units. Light and the eye come first, the eye being the first sense organ met; senses before structures, which widens from the sense organs to all structures (the rows' order). Placed last because the earth units must finish before social studies' hazards unit. 24 days: three rows and two required plant cases; outside programs give this content 21 to 33 percent of content time (2026-11-11 review), and 24 of 111 unit days is 22 percent | Candidate: a fruit cut open (seeds inside) as a second plant reproduction case | Never look at the sun, even in a mirror; no lasers. Reaction game: soft rulers, no hitting. Body structures shown as diagrams, no student's own body measured or compared; no reproductive organs of humans. Boundary: no cells, no colors reflected, no retina, no memory storage. Readiness day 93 on seeing and the sense/response part of grade 1 parts, response day 95. Retrieve materials-light from this year's day 18 and survival structure-function reasoning taught in grade 3, plus life cycles and variation. Cactus spines and thorns are studied through images only; no live specimens are handled. Stems cut by the adult only; food coloring stains, so covered tables. Pollen allergies checked before the dissection. Simulation to list: a light-ray box tool. Agent reads: sensitivity; safety (hands-on). Anchor source: the frame's own anchors A and B, named on one line so the unit plan reads them; OpenSciEd elementary unit 4.4, Structure and Function, anchors on flying squirrels (read 2026-11-14 at openscied.org) and was not used, since this unit's question is built on the cat and the plant. |

