| 1 | North America on the map. "What can a map tell us, and what does it leave out?" | 16 | ss4-map-scale; ss4-political-map | MA.4.T1.1; D2.Geo.3.3-5; MA.4.T1.2 | Owned: D2.Geo.3. Practiced: D1, D3.4, D4.1 | A physical map of North America with directions, scale, key and title: its rivers, lakes, oceans, mountains and deserts; distances with a ruler and parts of a scale bar, converted to miles or kilometres; the same place at three scales (town, state, continent) and what each shows and hides; Canada's provinces and territories, Mexico's states, the Caribbean nations and the United States on a political map; continent, nation, country, state, province, county and city (a city in a county in a state as a nested example; a nation told apart from a US state, Native nations among nations); places by latitude and longitude in whole degrees | Massachusetts opens grade 4 here, and every later unit maps on this continent: map-scale, map-key, latitude-longitude and region are taught first so units 3 to 7 can use them | Candidate: New York grade 5, the physical regions of the Western Hemisphere, read for one map task that places North America in the hemisphere | None beyond the lens's general rules. Native nations' lands shown on the physical map are named in the present |
| 2 | Our neighbors: Canada and Mexico. "How are Canada, Mexico and the United States tied together?" | 22 | ss4-canada-mexico-research; ss4-trade-north-america | MA.4.T1.3; D1.5.3-5; D3.1.3-5; D3.2.3-5; D4.5.3-5; D2.Eco.14.3-5; D2.Eco.15.3-5 | Owned: D1.5; D3.1, D3.2; D4.5; D2.Eco.14, 15. Practiced: D1.1 to D1.4, D3.4, D4.1 | Research Canada or Mexico (people and languages, government, cities, land, industries) from maps, atlases, articles and digital sources; choose sources that fit the question by two tests (does the set hold more than one view, and what do each source's origin, kind and date say), and how a source's setting (where, when and for whom it was made) affects how useful it is for the question; fact and opinion; check one fact in two sources; present, and critique a classmate's explanation for order, detail and accuracy. Why the three countries trade so much, what crosses the borders, and who gains and who loses in each | Follows the political map of unit 1 and keeps Massachusetts's order (4.T1.3). Trade sits here because this geography sets up the comparison (the row's own reason). The research routine taught here is used in every later unit | Candidate: New York grade 5, comparing the governments of Canada and Mexico with the United States (one short comparison table) | Indigenous peoples of each country (First Nations, Inuit and Métis; Indigenous peoples of Mexico) named in the present, from their own and their governments' pages. Trade's losers named without blaming a country |
| 3 | The first peoples of North America. "How do we know about people who lived here thousands of years ago?" | 18 | ss4-first-peoples-evidence; ss4-native-nations-sites | MA.4.T2.1; MA.4.T2.2; MA.4.T2.3; MA.4.T2.4 | Practiced: D2.His.1 (timeline), D3.4, D4.1, D1 | Competing theories of how people first reached North America (a land bridge, a coastal sea route) weighed against dated evidence of people here about 15,000 years ago or earlier; what digs, artifacts and climate data can and cannot show; a given timeline marked in thousands of years, on which students place two or three dates (they do not build it: the to-scale timeline is unit 4's, deep time grade 6's); Native culture regions on a map, and photographs of stone tools, pottery, mounds and cliff dwellings read for what they show about the societies that made them; Mesa Verde, Cahokia and other preserved places, and what they add to the full story of American life, and the nations who count their builders as ancestors and govern today (the Pueblo nations; the Osage and other nations for Cahokia) | Time order: the deep past comes before the voyages of unit 4. It needs unit 1's maps and scale, and it is the first unit where students write a claim weighed against evidence on a question with more than one defensible answer | Candidate: California grade 5, major pre-Columbian settlements (cliff dwellers, pueblo peoples, mound builders, Woodlands peoples) and how farming shaped them | Native nations' own accounts of their origins are named as their own, beside the archaeology, not ranked against it. These are ancestral places of living nations, not ruins of vanished peoples. Sacred or restricted knowledge left out. Flagged for the sensitivity read |
| 4 | Voyages and conquest. "What did the voyages of the 1400s and 1500s mean for the people already here?" | 20 | ss4-primary-source; ss4-exploration | MA.4.T3.1; D2.His.9.3-5; D2.His.11.3-5; MA.4.T3.2; MA.4.T3.3; D2.His.1.3-5 | Owned: D2.His.1, 9, 11. Practiced: D1, D3.4, D4.1 | How historians study voyages from archaeology, maps, drawings, journals and objects, and what each kind adds; before using a source, who made it, when, for whom and why; two sources on one landing compared, with the check that one did not copy the other. Norse voyages and the evidence of their meetings with Native peoples; European voyages of the 1400s and 1500s and why rulers paid for them; each told with the Native nations it met. The history set (item level, decided here): the Norse at L'Anse aux Meadows; Columbus and the Taíno; Cortés, his Native allies and the Mexica capital (1521); one northern voyage (Cartier); one Spanish entrada into what is now the United States (Coronado, 1540 to 1542). A class timeline of landings and conquests drawn to scale in centuries, with a paired timeline setting Coronado beside Cartier's third voyage (1541 to 1542) for one explicit comparison of things happening at the same time in different places, each with the Native peoples it met | Time order: after the first peoples, before the country's growth. Grade 3 met the later 1500s and 1600s explorers; this unit reaches back to the earlier voyages, so the to-scale timeline joins both. Sourcing is taught here because these are the year's first primary sources of the past | Candidates: California grade 5, navigation tools of the voyages (compass, astrolabe, ship design); New York grade 5, the exchange of plants, animals and diseases between the hemispheres | Conquest and enslavement named plainly with the human side (MA.4.T3): Columbus's men enslaved Taíno people, whose numbers collapsed from violence, forced labor and disease; the conquest of the Mexica capital. Taíno descendants and Nahua people today. No 