LEARNING Observations that give evidence energy moves by sound (a drum makes rice on another drum jump), light (sunlight warms a dark cloth), heat (a warm hand warms a cold spoon) and electric current (a battery, wires and a bulb or buzzer in a closed circuit); the arrows extended through all four. Current gets its own hands-on days (about three: light a bulb, trace the closed path, add a switch) before the build, so every student has wired a circuit whatever device a team later picks. A device that changes energy from one form to another, designed, tested and refined: the class states criteria and constraints, compares first proposals, builds, finds where it fails and improves it. Named pick: a lid alarm (a circuit that sounds a buzzer when a box lid opens); the clarification's other examples (a rubber-band car using stored energy, a passive solar heater) are offered as team choices. Then the question the device raises: where does the current in the wall come from? Energy and fuels from natural resources: renewable (wind, water behind dams, sunlight) and nonrenewable (coal, oil, natural gas, and the fuel of nuclear power plants), and how using them affects the environment (habitat lost to dams and surface mines, air pollution from burning fossil fuels), from texts and the class's own state's energy data, shown as a bar graph or picture graph ordered by size ("of every 10 lights lit in our state, about 4 run on natural gas"), with no percent and no number over 1,000,000
UNIT 4 The changing land. "How did a river make a canyon a mile deep, and what do its layers tell us?" Anchor: the Grand Canyon: a small river at the bottom of a canyon a mile deep, and the same stripes of rock on both walls (a teacher may swap in a local road cut or river bank) days 17 
SKILLS s4-weathering-erosion; s4-rock-layers
LEARNING The canyon question first goes outside, to change the class can see: the bare patch under a downspout after a hard rain (a channel and a fan of sand, the grass beside it untouched) and a cracked step or a path lifted by a root, sketched and kept apart as erosion and weathering (a photograph set where the class cannot go out). Observations and measurements of weathering (breaking rock into smaller pieces: freezing and thawing a wet clay or plaster block; shaking rocks in a jar) and of the rate of erosion (carrying the pieces away) by water, ice, wind and plants: a sloped tray of sand with slope, water amount or plant cover changed one at a time; data in tables and bar graphs. The two words never blurred. Rainfall shapes the land and affects what can live there; living things change the land (roots crack rock, burrows move soil). Then rock layers and their fossils as evidence of how a landscape changed, opened by a retrieval task on grade 3's fossils: shell fossils in a layer above plant fossils show land became sea; the canyon shows a river cut through the rock over a long time; lower layers in undisturbed rock are older (relative time); a layer model of modeling clay with shells or buttons buried as fossils, cut and read, then pushed from both ends so it bends and breaks; one real case of bent or broken layers read as evidence of earth forces. Nature of science: the record is read by assuming the same processes worked then as now
